From 367d19035c0d2137a36d9f9d3286d42ecc356de3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: MikiMiku <jonathanirvinjo@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 18:46:17 +0700
Subject: [PATCH] antinomie: record pages written through shared file mappings

A store into a writable shared mapping of a file changes the file
without a system call, so the host's history of the file, rebuilt from
the calls that wrote it, misses it entirely. The kernel takes note of
such stores only through the page table entry's dirty bit, which it
consumes in two places: when the page is written back, and when the
mapping goes away. Both are where the pages are recorded, with their
content at that moment.

A lifecycle record of the call, lifecycle.file_mapped
(antinomie.filemap/1, 96 bytes per range), says which pages of which
file, why (WRITEBACK: the call wrote them back, as msync, fsync or sync
do; UNMAP: the call removed a mapping that held them dirty, as munmap,
mremap, exec or exit do), the file size and time, and a write order;
the bytes follow as lifecycle.file_mapped.data. A call's records are one
event published just before its exit. The address space a task drops
when it exits has no exit of a call to go with: its pages go out just
before lifecycle.task_exit, under the task's last call.

The copies are made where the kernel sees the page, under the page
table lock or the folio lock, so they never sleep: one page each from a
nonblocking allocation, at most 4 MiB per call, at most 64 ranges per
call. A range past the budget, or without memory, is still recorded,
with the reason its bytes are missing; pages past the ranges are
counted.

Each range takes its write order from the counter writes use, while
holding the file's inode lock shared with a trylock, and every page of
the range is copied while holding it: every write with a lower number
is complete in the copy and none with a higher number has begun. When
a write holds the lock, the range has no number.

Only the result of the stores is seen, not each store. A page the
background writeback wrote while the mapping stayed, and that was not
stored to again, is clean when the mapping goes and is not recorded.
Mappings of the kernel's internal files (shared anonymous memory,
System V shared memory, memfd, AIO rings) are not followed.

Hooked kernel files:
- mm/memory.c: zap_present_folio_ptes(), where a dirty entry of a file
  page moves its dirty bit to the folio as the mapping is removed.
- mm/page-writeback.c: folio_clear_dirty_for_io(), where the mappings
  of a folio about to be written back are cleaned and found dirty.

Contract: revision 18.
